Reconstruct the complete finite investigation, keeping its fixed inputs, intended physical mean, random error law and observation units explicit. Use [Investigate inconsistent readings lost directions and projected fits](../../mathematics/core/Investigate inconsistent readings lost directions and projected fits.lingua#r_005B6E212C56EB47DF7702079B) for the unchanged exact elimination program `linear_space_study.py`. Save that program beside the two original programs below. The study solves the two normal equations through shared elimination; the companion independently uses centred scalar sums and pair contrasts. No source dataset or external program is bundled.

The initial 32-state model yields E(α̂)=2, E(β̂)=3, Var(α̂)=1/5, Var(β̂)=1/10, Cov=0 and E(SSE)=3. Check every residual covariance against I−H, not just the diagonal. Then compare the fixed-offset, common-random-offset and curvature rivals, and the replicated-data partition. The interval count denominators are 32 for a fixed mean and 64 when an independent future error is also varied.

`fit` requires at least three paired exact observations and at least two distinct input levels because it also returns s². General least squares can be meaningful under other rank/sample conditions; this particular interface deliberately rejects them. Integers and Fractions are accepted, while floats, booleans and mismatched shapes are rejected. Exact arithmetic avoids rounding in this bounded teaching study; it is not a recommendation to solve all large floating-point problems through normal equations.

Run `nix shell nixpkgs#python3 -c python3 fitting_checks.py` from the directory containing all three programs. Inspect outputs and failures before altering the inputs. The independent scalar formulas, error-state enumeration and replication identity check different aspects of the implementation. General proofs in the preceding lessons supply claims beyond the finite checked cases.

